<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tulsidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1532–<a href="/years/1623">1623</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tulsi Das, Tulasidasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tulsidas was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> poet-saint of 16th-century northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, devoted to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, who spent most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> schooled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, he is best known for the Ramcharitmanas, a retelling of the Sanskrit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in the vernacular Awadhi, which he began at Ayodhya in 1574. Popular belief also makes him the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a>, and he is credited with founding the Sankat Mochan Hanuman Temple in Varanasi and with starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> plays, a folk-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> version of the Ramayana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tulsidas.jpg" alt="Tulsidas" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nawal Kishore Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustration_of_Tuls%C4%ABd%C4%81s_holding_a_M%C4%81nas_manuscript_in_front_of_an_audience,_Naval_Kishore_edition,_1882.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…ascribed to the poet Valmiki, tells of the exile of Prince Rama, the abduction of his wife Sita by the demon king Ravana and her rescue; retold in countless versions, among them the Ramcharitmanas of <b>Tulsidas</b> in the 16th century, it is performed across South and Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tulsidas</b> recorded in the Ramcharitmanas itself that he began the poem at Ayodhya in 1574, on the festival of Rama’s birth; the Ramlila plays still staged across northern India each autumn are based on its verses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Brahmin priests and scholars performed the rites of temple and household and kept Sanskrit learning alive; the Brahmin poet <b>Tulsidas</b> retold the Ramayana in Awadhi.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hanuman Chalisa, &quot;forty chaupais on Hanuman&quot;, is a Hindu devotional hymn (stotra) in praise of Hanuman, written in Awadhi by <b>Tulsidas</b>, a poet-saint of the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a></li><li><blockquote>It is believed to have been written by the poet <b>Tulsidas</b> or drawn from his Ramcharitmanas, or to come from a 17th-century sung prayer by the Marathi saint-poet Ramdas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/raghupati-raghava-raja-ram">Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram</a></li></ul></section>
